Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "$ If the return value is non-negative, new array list contains x ");
</code>
In the given code block, it is given as "@@@ If the return value is non-negative, new array list contains x". So we would get the Debug.Assert statement as "Debug.Assert (New_Ret >= 0, "$ If the return value is non-negative, new array list contains x")". The 'New_Ret' variable represents the resultant count of the array list and the second argument in debug.Assert is a message to be displayed when the condition doesn't get satisfied. 

It would follow similar lines for each @@@ comment in your original code. Make sure you replace 'New_Ret' with the appropriate variable representing your new result. Parsing comments to extract this information is typically not performed, as it is more accurately handled by the C# code itself. If there are additional conditions that need to be met, you can include those in the parentheses of the Assert.Fails as well. 

Note: Ensure you import the System.Diagnostics namespace and replace the 'Debug' with the 'System.Diagnostics.Debug' to make the assertion work. 

Also, this line is for IntelliJ Idea or any other IDE. Make sure you use equivalent framework in your target language. These assertions are used to check for incorrect program behavior at runtime.--------------------
